1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:07,446
Metralign localizes a fine reference field inside a wider periodic inspection image.

2
00:00:08,306 --> 00:00:13,576
It is deterministic, training-free, and returns one subpixel coordinate.

3
00:00:14,836 --> 00:00:20,951
The reference spans only a small fraction of the physical width represented by the search image.

4
00:00:21,711 --> 00:00:27,592
In a repeated array, many locations appear locally correct, so ordinary registration is not enough.

5
00:00:28,552 --> 00:00:31,697
The actual task is absolute-site localization.

6
00:00:33,057 --> 00:00:39,063
A normalized template correlator produces a family of strong periodic maxima.

7
00:00:39,823 --> 00:00:46,714
Near-competing periodic maxima demonstrate that local appearance alone cannot certify the absolute site.

8
00:00:48,074 --> 00:00:52,816
Metralign first measures periodic structure in reciprocal and phase space.

9
00:00:53,576 --> 00:01:01,088
Peak spacing and phase drift estimate the sampling ratio, bounded rotation, and real-space pitch.

10
00:01:01,848 --> 00:01:06,101
These are measured from the selected sample, not drawn for illustration.

11
00:01:07,361 --> 00:01:11,692
The same one-period spatial difference is then applied to both acquisitions.

12
00:01:12,452 --> 00:01:18,840
Repeated structure is attenuated; persistent site-specific variation remains.

13
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:25,890
Matched processing makes the residual evidence comparable across the two sampling paths.

14
00:01:27,150 --> 00:01:32,660
Correlation ranks supported local maxima while retaining bounded alternative hypotheses.

15
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:41,138
A center or supplied stage prior can break a tie only when residual support is low or the transform is unstable.

16
00:01:41,998 --> 00:01:47,888
The result carries its score, ambiguity evidence, alternatives, and a review recommendation.

17
00:01:49,148 --> 00:01:52,880
Finally a parabolic fit refines the selected integer peak.

18
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:59,365
On this benchmark example, the prediction error is 0.094 pixels.

19
00:02:00,825 --> 00:02:05,511
This is the real command executed against the checked-in sample.

20
00:02:06,371 --> 00:02:12,430
Standard output gives exactly the predicted x and y coordinate. Full diagnostics remain separate.

21
00:02:13,890 --> 00:02:20,310
The frozen test set contains 1400 synthetic pairs across 7 fixed stress conditions.

22
00:02:21,070 --> 00:02:27,617
Only 2 predictions exceeded 1 pixel; median error was 0.083 pixels.

23
00:02:28,377 --> 00:02:35,808
The ninety-fifth percentile was 0.319 pixels, with mean localization wall time of 239 milliseconds.

24
00:02:36,968 --> 00:02:45,714
Across 6 fixed classic adapters, the best, OpenCV grid template, reached 35.57 percent within 1 pixel.

25
00:02:46,874 --> 00:02:57,784
A separate official XFeat comparison returned 0 locations within 5 pixels. Because it uses different task assumptions, this is not a verdict on its intended benchmarks.

26
00:02:59,244 --> 00:03:09,675
Separate checks use acquired SEM and TEM records, plus a separately implemented synthetic renderer with independent code but shared task-level geometry assumptions.

27
00:03:10,435 --> 00:03:17,904
Their protocols, compatibility paths, licenses, and ground-truth limits remain separate from the main benchmark result.

28
00:03:19,364 --> 00:03:27,043
The frozen test set contains 2 large errors in highly repetitive line-array cases; both were flagged ambiguous.

29
00:03:27,803 --> 00:03:38,042
Weak site-specific evidence left many plausible absolute positions; this case returned an incorrect location under the prescribed tie-breaking rule.

30
00:03:39,502 --> 00:03:47,506
The repository includes generation, inference, evaluation, complete reports, input hashes, and reproducible build commands.

31
00:03:48,366 --> 00:03:54,005
Metralign is available at github dot com slash Achxy slash metralign.
